    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b1027660-b4d7-554c-ae62-ff804cacf944",
      ":question/problem": "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{2 x^{2} - y^{2} - 1}{\\sqrt{y}},\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ at the point $(-1,4)$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Hold $x$ constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Write $N=2 x^{2} - y^{2} - 1$ and $D=\\sqrt{y}$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{y}&=- 2 y, \\\\\nD_{y}&=\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{y}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the square root use $D_{y}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}$.  Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{N_{y}D-ND_{y}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(- 2 y\\right)D-N\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{y}}\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{- 2 x^{2} - 3 y^{2} + 1}{2\\left(y\\right)\\sqrt{y}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step multiplies the numerator and denominator by $2\\sqrt{y}$ and collects the numerator terms. Now substitute the point, after differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\right|_{(-1,4)}&=\\frac{\\left(-8\\right)\\left(2\\right)-\\left(-15\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}{\\left(2\\right)^2} \\\\\n&=- \\frac{49}{16}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n These expressions apply where $y>0$.",
